.TH COUNTFS 4
.SH NAME
countfs \- counter file service (tutorial example)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.BI "mount {countfs} " dir
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Countfs
serves 9P on its standard input, presenting a small file tree that
maintains a single integer counter.  It exists as the worked example
for the file-server tutorial,
.BR docs/TUTORIAL-9P-SERVICE.md ,
and is deliberately minimal: three files, text data, access control
by file mode.
.PP
The served tree contains:
.TP
.B ctl
Write-only (mode 222).  Accepts one textual request per write:
.RS
.TP
.BI add " n"
add
.I n
to the counter
.TP
.BI set " n"
set the counter to
.I n
.TP
.B reset
set the counter to zero
.RE
.IP
Any other request fails with an error.  Reads are rejected by the
file mode.
.TP
.B value
Read-only (mode 444).  The current counter value as decimal text,
one line.
.TP
.B log
Read-only (mode 444).  One line per change, oldest first, at most 64
lines, fields space-separated:
.IP
.EX
.I time verb old new
.EE
.IP
where
.I time
is an RFC 3339 UTC timestamp.  The log is line-oriented so that
.IR grep (1)
and pipelines apply directly.
.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
mount {countfs} /mnt/count
echo add 5 > /mnt/count/ctl
cat /mnt/count/value        # prints 5
cat /mnt/count/log
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/countfs.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR mount (1),
.IR styxservers (2),
.IR mntgen (4),
.B docs/TUTORIAL-9P-SERVICE.md
.SH BUGS
The counter and log are held in memory and vanish with the process;
persistence, were it wanted, would come from mounting the service
over a durable path, not from code.
